            "passage": "THE REPAIR CAFÉ\nWhen Hannah Clarke's vacuum cleaner stopped working two years ago, the shop told her it would cost more to repair it than to buy a new one. \"I hated the idea of throwing it away,\" she says. \"So I watched a few videos online, opened it up and found that a small belt had broken. The new part cost three pounds.\"\nThat experience gave Hannah, a 27-year-old nurse from Leeds, an idea. She persuaded the manager of her local community centre to let her use a room on the first Saturday of every month, and she put up posters asking for volunteers who were good at fixing things. Six people came to the first meeting. Now there are more than twenty, including a retired electrician, two students who repair laptops and a woman who can mend almost any piece of clothing.\nVisitors bring their broken items and sit with a volunteer while the repair is done, so they can learn how to do it themselves next time. The service is free, although most people leave a small donation, which pays for tea, cake and spare parts. Not everything can be saved – about one item in five is beyond repair – but last year the café fixed over 600 things that would otherwise have ended up in the rubbish.\n\"People think it's all about saving money, and that's part of it,\" Hannah explains. \"But for me, the best thing is that neighbours who would never normally meet are sitting together, chatting and learning from each other.\"",
