                "text": "Keeping staff is cheaper than replacing those who leave."
              }
            ],
            "passage": "A FOUR-DAY WEEK: ONE YEAR ON\nWhen a mid-sized software company in Leeds announced last year that its staff would work four days a week for the same salary, many observers predicted chaos. Twelve months on, the results are more mixed than either supporters or critics expected.\nAccording to the company's own figures, output remained roughly stable, and sick leave fell by almost a third. Staff turnover, which had been a persistent problem, dropped to its lowest level in a decade. \"People simply stopped leaving,\" says operations director Priya Nair. \"Recruiting and training a replacement costs us far more than the extra day off.\"\nHowever, the transition was not painless. Customer-facing teams struggled at first, because clients still expected replies on Fridays. The company eventually solved this by splitting those teams into two groups that take different days off, an arrangement Nair admits \"took months to get right\". Some employees also reported that the shorter week made their remaining days more intense, with fewer breaks and less time for informal conversation.\nResearchers who studied the trial warn against drawing sweeping conclusions. Dr Tom Wheeler, who led the independent evaluation, points out that the firm was already profitable and that its work is largely done on computers, which makes it easier to measure. \"What works for a software company may not work for a hospital or a building site,\" he says. Nevertheless, he believes the trial shows that working hours deserve to be questioned rather than accepted as a fixed part of working life.\nThe company has now made the four-day week permanent, although managers will review it every year.",
