        {
          "answer": "o26260068",
          "content_hash": "0cf5d0b6bb12548cd7dbe35cdbe81f7c4d0e087f20fb9251c856284b1687eaee",
          "explanation": {
            "vi": "Bà Okafor giải thích đồng hồ sinh học của thiếu niên bị lùi lại, hoóc-môn gây buồn ngủ tiết ra muộn hơn người lớn khoảng hai tiếng (“released roughly two hours later”). Phương án b là bẫy: hoóc-môn tiết ra muộn hơn chứ không phải ít hơn; a là bẫy vì điện thoại có được nhắc, nhưng chỉ ở phần nói về giới hạn của việc vào học muộn."
          },
          "id": "25795da0-ff84-503f-b092-2186b8a0f700",
          "key": "en:examq:cefr-c1-m1-l03",
          "kind": "mcq",
          "level_code": "b2",
          "points": 1,
          "prompt": {
            "audio_asset_id": null,
            "instruction_vi": "Nghe đoạn phỏng vấn trên đài phát thanh và chọn đáp án đúng.",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "ocf985bcf",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They produce less of the hormone that causes sleepiness."
              },
              {
                "id": "oa988e7fd",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They stay up too late using their phones."
              },
              {
                "id": "o72059900",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They are less motivated by school than younger children."
              },
              {
                "id": "o26260068",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Their internal clock runs later than an adult's."
              }
            ],
            "stem": "According to Dr Okafor, why do teenagers find early mornings difficult?",
            "stem_lang": "en"
          },
          "question_type": "cefr_listen_detail",
          "rubric": null,
          "sample_answer": null,
          "script": "Presenter: Dr Okafor, people often say teenagers are simply lazy in the mornings. Is there any truth in that?\nDr Okafor: Very little, I'm afraid. During adolescence the body clock shifts, so the hormone that makes us feel sleepy is released roughly two hours later than it is in adults. Asking a fifteen-year-old to be alert at eight a.m. is a bit like asking an adult to concentrate at six.\nPresenter: So should schools start later?\nDr Okafor: The evidence points that way, although I'd be wary of presenting it as a cure-all. Schools that have moved their start time back by an hour do report better attendance, but the gains in exam results are modest, and they tend to disappear if students simply use the extra hour to stay up later on their phones.",
          "section": "listening",
          "seq": 3
        },
        {
          "answer": "o289abd3f",
          "content_hash": "d268b7adafe33e591b8016a34c126924355da7e82cd0a8cb1e1a7e903e42dff6",
          "explanation": {
            "vi": "“The evidence points that way” (bằng chứng ủng hộ) nhưng “I'd be wary of presenting it as a cure-all” (tôi dè dặt khi coi đó là thuốc chữa bách bệnh): bà ủng hộ có chừng mực. Phương án c sai vì tỉ lệ đi học có cải thiện và điểm thi tăng “modest” (khiêm tốn) chứ không phải bằng không; a ngược hẳn với chữ modest."
          },
          "id": "78e7120d-ab28-5f03-bd80-19d7505cce7c",
          "key": "en:examq:cefr-c1-m1-l04",
          "kind": "mcq",
          "level_code": "c1",
          "points": 1,
          "prompt": {
            "audio_asset_id": null,
            "instruction_vi": "Nghe lại đoạn phỏng vấn và chọn câu mô tả đúng quan điểm của người được phỏng vấn.",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "o289abd3f",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They are helpful, but their benefits should not be exaggerated."
              },
              {
                "id": "o1bcec115",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They should not be introduced until more research is done."
              },
              {
                "id": "o4468758a",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They have no measurable effect on students."
              },
              {
                "id": "obd8feece",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They are the most effective way to raise exam results."
              }
            ],
            "stem": "What is Dr Okafor's view of later school start times?",
            "stem_lang": "en"
          },
          "question_type": "cefr_listen_attitude",
          "rubric": null,
          "sample_answer": null,
          "script": "Presenter: Dr Okafor, people often say teenagers are simply lazy in the mornings. Is there any truth in that?\nDr Okafor: Very little, I'm afraid. During adolescence the body clock shifts, so the hormone that makes us feel sleepy is released roughly two hours later than it is in adults. Asking a fifteen-year-old to be alert at eight a.m. is a bit like asking an adult to concentrate at six.\nPresenter: So should schools start later?\nDr Okafor: The evidence points that way, although I'd be wary of presenting it as a cure-all. Schools that have moved their start time back by an hour do report better attendance, but the gains in exam results are modest, and they tend to disappear if students simply use the extra hour to stay up later on their phones.",
          "section": "listening",
          "seq": 4
        },
        {
          "answer": "oeab19037",
          "content_hash": "6b0246acabb0728896f6bfb9d94ae355b975943d33aa7ff05a0df210933bd0f5",
          "explanation": {
            "vi": "Giảng viên nói dạng mạnh của giả thuyết đã gần như bị bỏ (“largely abandoned”), nhưng một nhận định khiêm tốn hơn vẫn tồn tại (“What has survived is a far more modest claim”), và câu hỏi nay là ngôn ngữ ảnh hưởng “bao nhiêu”. Phương án a quá tuyệt đối: “largely” không phải “completely”, và một phần ý tưởng vẫn đứng vững; b chính là dạng mạnh đã bị bác bỏ."
          },
          "id": "9723a8da-00a1-5157-8ee5-d3a9ecf66699",
          "key": "en:examq:cefr-c1-m1-l05",
          "kind": "mcq",
          "level_code": "c1",
          "points": 1,
          "prompt": {
            "audio_asset_id": null,
            "instruction_vi": "Nghe đoạn trích bài giảng và chọn ý chính.",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "o74eaa2dd",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Whorf's ideas have been completely disproved."
              },
              {
                "id": "obf96fb46",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Languages without words for left and right are harder to learn."
              },
              {
                "id": "oeab19037",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "A weaker version of the hypothesis is still taken seriously."
              },
              {
                "id": "oa8663087",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Language determines which concepts people are able to understand."
              }
            ],
            "stem": "What is the main point of this part of the lecture?",
            "stem_lang": "en"
          },
          "question_type": "cefr_listen_gist",
          "rubric": null,
          "sample_answer": null,
          "script": "Lecturer: The idea that the language we speak shapes the way we think is usually associated with Benjamin Lee Whorf. In its strong form – that language determines thought, so that speakers simply cannot conceive of anything their language has no word for – the hypothesis has been largely abandoned. After all, we're perfectly capable of grasping a new concept the first time someone explains it to us. What has survived is a far more modest claim: that language can nudge our attention and memory in particular directions. Speakers of languages that describe location using compass directions rather than \"left\" and \"right\", for instance, turn out to be remarkably good at keeping track of which way they are facing, even indoors. The question researchers now ask is not whether language influences thought, but how much, and under what conditions.",
          "section": "listening",
          "seq": 5
        },
        {
          "answer": "o7ded084f",
          "content_hash": "21094a4fe190f0aa541a35597e48fde1e55a86c7fdd8e731f491b4daa38322d1",
          "explanation": {
            "vi": "“Remarkably good at keeping track of which way they are facing, even indoors” nghĩa là họ luôn biết rõ mình đang quay về hướng nào, kể cả trong nhà. Phương án c đảo ngược ý (“even indoors” là vẫn giỏi chứ không phải bị lạc); b lặp lại dạng mạnh đã bị bác bỏ; d khái quát quá mức vì bài chỉ nói về trí nhớ định hướng."
          },
          "id": "5dca0738-811b-5b1d-b653-af031336fe6c",
          "key": "en:examq:cefr-c1-m1-l06",
          "kind": "mcq",
          "level_code": "c1",
          "points": 1,
          "prompt": {
            "audio_asset_id": null,
            "instruction_vi": "Nghe lại đoạn trích bài giảng và chọn đáp án đúng.",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "odd2fc329",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They have better memories than other speakers in general."
              },
              {
                "id": "o7ded084f",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They are unusually aware of which way they are facing."
              },
              {
                "id": "oebc04dd4",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They easily lose their sense of direction indoors."
              },
              {
                "id": "o5c378acb",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They cannot understand the ideas of left and right."
              }
            ],
            "stem": "What does the lecturer say about speakers of languages that use compass directions?",
            "stem_lang": "en"
          },
          "question_type": "cefr_listen_detail",
          "rubric": null,
          "sample_answer": null,
          "script": "Lecturer: The idea that the language we speak shapes the way we think is usually associated with Benjamin Lee Whorf. In its strong form – that language determines thought, so that speakers simply cannot conceive of anything their language has no word for – the hypothesis has been largely abandoned. After all, we're perfectly capable of grasping a new concept the first time someone explains it to us. What has survived is a far more modest claim: that language can nudge our attention and memory in particular directions. Speakers of languages that describe location using compass directions rather than \"left\" and \"right\", for instance, turn out to be remarkably good at keeping track of which way they are facing, even indoors. The question researchers now ask is not whether language influences thought, but how much, and under what conditions.",
          "section": "listening",
          "seq": 6
        },
        {
          "answer": "o2743fdc0",
          "content_hash": "6444ce8db8408fed52ed9e2b4f90be476ffdd7b98a567c7cc4e3cb1a2e881381",
          "explanation": {
            "vi": "Người nữ lo về những điều học được “tình cờ” — nhân viên trẻ nghe lỏm cách người có kinh nghiệm xử lý khách khó — tức là kèm cặp không chính thức, và cái giá này vài năm sau mới lộ ra nên không ai liên hệ lại (“nobody connects it to the decision”). Phương án a là bẫy vì số email chỉ là ví dụ về thứ dễ đo; b sai vì cô nói các nghiên cứu cho kết quả trái ngược nhau."
          },
          "id": "ce119cff-78f6-5c0e-a9c1-1d93704ccf6d",
          "key": "en:examq:cefr-c1-m1-l07",
          "kind": "mcq",
          "level_code": "c1",
          "points": 1,
          "prompt": {
            "audio_asset_id": null,
            "instruction_vi": "Nghe đoạn trích podcast và chọn đáp án đúng.",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "o5232364a",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Remote workers send too many emails."
              },
              {
                "id": "o6292ae63",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Remote work always reduces productivity."
              },
              {
                "id": "o2743fdc0",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Its effect on informal learning may go unnoticed."
              },
              {
                "id": "o3117be22",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Senior staff refuse to mentor colleagues online."
              }
            ],
            "stem": "What is the woman's main concern about remote work?",
            "stem_lang": "en"
          },
          "question_type": "cefr_listen_attitude",
          "rubric": null,
          "sample_answer": null,
          "script": "Woman: What strikes me about the remote-work debate is how quickly it became tribal. You're either for the office or against it.\nMan: Partly because the early studies were so contradictory. One found productivity went up, another that it collapsed.\nWoman: But they were often measuring different things. If you count emails sent or tasks closed, remote workers look fantastic. What's much harder to measure is the stuff that happens by accident – the junior colleague who overhears how someone senior handles a difficult client.\nMan: Mentoring, essentially.\nWoman: Exactly. And that cost doesn't show up for a couple of years, by which point nobody connects it to the decision to go remote.\nMan: So you'd haul everyone back into the office?\nWoman: Not at all. I'd just like companies to be honest that they're making a trade-off, rather than pretending there's such a thing as a free lunch.",
          "section": "listening",
          "seq": 7
        },
        {
          "answer": "o21bf184e",
          "content_hash": "9377d8799a27f1937b69c7c2172374e738f1487d6ff10b7e6e35bddb8dc21058",
          "explanation": {
            "vi": "Câu chốt “be honest that they're making a trade-off” (thừa nhận là đang đánh đổi) và thành ngữ “there's no such thing as a free lunch” (không có gì miễn phí) cho thấy cô muốn doanh nghiệp nói rõ cái giá phải trả. Phương án a bị cô bác thẳng (“Not at all”); d sai vì cô chỉ nói các nghiên cứu đo những thứ khác nhau, không hề nói họ cố tình đánh lừa."
          },
          "id": "39d39a6d-ac3b-512d-a6e3-ee5b0c14edb6",
          "key": "en:examq:cefr-c1-m1-l08",
          "kind": "mcq",
          "level_code": "c1",
          "points": 1,
          "prompt": {
            "audio_asset_id": null,
            "instruction_vi": "Nghe lại đoạn trích podcast và chọn câu tóm tắt đúng nhất quan điểm của người nữ.",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "o21bf184e",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Choosing remote work involves costs that companies should acknowledge."
              },
              {
                "id": "ofa1e8cb3",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Early research on remote work was deliberately misleading."
              },
              {
                "id": "obe688c5d",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Companies should require all staff to return to the office."
              },
              {
                "id": "o1b4e356b",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Remote work has no real disadvantages for experienced staff."
              }
            ],
            "stem": "Which statement best summarises the woman's position?",
            "stem_lang": "en"
          },
          "question_type": "cefr_listen_gist",
          "rubric": null,
          "sample_answer": null,
          "script": "Woman: What strikes me about the remote-work debate is how quickly it became tribal. You're either for the office or against it.\nMan: Partly because the early studies were so contradictory. One found productivity went up, another that it collapsed.\nWoman: But they were often measuring different things. If you count emails sent or tasks closed, remote workers look fantastic. What's much harder to measure is the stuff that happens by accident – the junior colleague who overhears how someone senior handles a difficult client.\nMan: Mentoring, essentially.\nWoman: Exactly. And that cost doesn't show up for a couple of years, by which point nobody connects it to the decision to go remote.\nMan: So you'd haul everyone back into the office?\nWoman: Not at all. I'd just like companies to be honest that they're making a trade-off, rather than pretending there's such a thing as a free lunch.",
          "section": "listening",
          "seq": 8
        },
        {
          "answer": "oc4cbb0f5",
          "content_hash": "be4a5ef76d5a9aa6488976955f027e33afd530cfab9300094242f84d49eef0e8",
          "explanation": {
            "vi": "Bài nói chính bị lùi sang 11 giờ, và vào lúc 9:30 buổi thảo luận về tài trợ nghiên cứu sẽ diễn ra “in Room B rather than the main hall”. Phương án b là bẫy vì nghe thấy “main hall”, nhưng hội trường chính đang được chuẩn bị cho buổi chiều; a là lịch cũ (“originally scheduled”)."
          },
          "id": "82bce877-32fa-5f77-8fbc-8e109cb0aafe",
          "key": "en:examq:cefr-c1-m1-l09",
          "kind": "mcq",
          "level_code": "b2",
          "points": 1,
          "prompt": {
            "audio_asset_id": null,
            "instruction_vi": "Nghe thông báo tại một hội nghị và chọn đáp án đúng.",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "o81a9907a",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Professor Lindqvist will give her talk in the main hall."
              },
              {
                "id": "o481eb4c7",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Two of the workshops will begin."
