          "content_hash": "6a2bd76300949e02c4c60e5b7eae4957a8c4dedb36cd6c176efb085e473954cb",
          "explanation": {
            "vi": "Bản đồ cho thấy loài vật “nhiều” gần thành phố và “ít” ở vùng xa “not because of any genuine difference in population, but because few observers ever go there”, tức là bản đồ phản ánh nơi con người hay đến chứ không chỉ nơi loài sinh sống. Phương án a nhầm sai lệch dữ liệu với sự di cư thật; c sai vì bài nói có thể hiệu chỉnh, chỉ là cần kỹ thuật thống kê phức tạp hơn."
          },
          "id": "da1b8cae-15c6-5e9a-a565-6d08f01f107b",
          "key": "en:examq:cefr-c1-m1-r10",
          "kind": "mcq",
          "level_code": "c1",
          "points": 1,
          "prompt": {
            "audio_asset_id": null,
            "instruction_vi": "Đọc bài viết và chọn điều có thể suy ra từ nội dung.",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "odbdc4a48",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "Their errors cannot be corrected by any method."
              },
              {
                "id": "o2da6426c",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They may reflect where people go as much as where species live."
              },
              {
                "id": "o3512d6ca",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They show that many species are moving towards cities."
              },
              {
                "id": "oe78c59fe",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They are more accurate for remote areas than for cities."
              }
            ],
            "passage": "Citizen science – the involvement of non-professionals in scientific research – is often celebrated as a straightforward good. Volunteers count birds, classify galaxies from telescope images or record the first flowering of plants each spring, and in doing so they generate data on a scale no university department could afford. Yet professional scientists have not always welcomed these contributions, and their reservations deserve to be taken seriously rather than dismissed as snobbery.\nThe most frequently voiced concern is data quality. An untrained observer may confuse two similar species or record a sighting in the wrong place. In practice, however, this problem has proved more manageable than critics predicted. Well-designed projects ask several volunteers to classify each item independently and treat only their consensus as reliable; studies comparing such pooled judgements with expert assessments have frequently found the two to be almost indistinguishable.\nA subtler difficulty concerns where, rather than how well, volunteers observe. People tend to record wildlife close to their homes, along popular footpaths and at weekends. The resulting maps can show a species as abundant near cities and scarce in remote uplands, not because of any genuine difference in population, but because few observers ever go there. Correcting for this kind of bias requires statistical techniques that are considerably more demanding than simply checking individual records.\nNone of this suggests that citizen science should be scaled back. It does suggest that its value depends less on the enthusiasm of its volunteers than on the care with which projects are designed and their data interpreted.",
            "stem": "What can be inferred about maps based on volunteers' records?",
            "stem_lang": "en"
          },
          "question_type": "cefr_read_inference",
          "rubric": null,
          "sample_answer": null,
          "script": null,
          "section": "reading",
          "seq": 20
        },
        {
          "answer": "o7a7cc3b0",
          "content_hash": "7c2924ab180d1995c7f506cee9da76c46acf1d096aed71043630db299f895c32",
          "explanation": {
            "vi": "“About as well on routine exercises… and noticeably better… to unfamiliar problems”: ngang bằng ở bài tập quen thuộc, tốt hơn rõ rệt ở bài lạ. Phương án a khái quát quá mức; b sai ở vế đầu (“about as well” là ngang bằng chứ không kém hơn); d ngược với “Most of them fail to reach the correct answer”."
          },
          "id": "0d49d3c3-6f45-5070-8fed-17448b72e3cf",
          "key": "en:examq:cefr-c1-m1-r11",
          "kind": "mcq",
          "level_code": "c1",
          "points": 1,
          "prompt": {
            "audio_asset_id": null,
            "instruction_vi": "Đọc bài bình luận về giáo dục và chọn đáp án đúng cho câu hỏi.",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "o5bd07d3d",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They usually manage to solve the opening problem."
              },
              {
                "id": "o65730663",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They perform worse on routine tasks but better on unfamiliar ones."
              },
              {
                "id": "o7a7cc3b0",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They perform similarly on routine tasks and better on unfamiliar ones."
              },
              {
                "id": "oc5c048ad",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "They perform better on every type of task."
              }
            ],
            "passage": "For most of the last century, the standard model of teaching mathematics was simple: explain the method, demonstrate it with an example, then let students practise. A growing number of education researchers now argue that this sequence may be back to front. Under an approach known as \"productive failure\", students are first given a challenging problem they have not been taught how to solve. Most of them fail to reach the correct answer, often spectacularly. Only then does the teacher present the standard method.\nThe counter-intuitive claim is that the initial struggle is not wasted time. By attempting the problem on their own, students draw on what they already know, discover the limits of that knowledge and come to understand what a good solution would need to achieve. When the formal method is finally introduced, it answers a question they have already been asking. Several controlled studies have found that students taught in this way perform about as well on routine exercises as those taught conventionally, and noticeably better when asked to apply the idea to unfamiliar problems.\nEnthusiasts, however, sometimes overlook how much depends on the design of the opening task. A problem that is too easy leads to premature success and little reflection; one that is too hard leaves students with nothing to build on and simply confirms their belief that they are \"bad at maths\". The failure, in other words, has to be the right kind of failure. That is a demanding standard, and one that cannot be met by simply withholding explanations and hoping for the best.",
            "stem": "According to the text, how do students taught through \"productive failure\" compare with those taught conventionally?",
            "stem_lang": "en"
          },
          "question_type": "cefr_read_detail",
          "rubric": null,
          "sample_answer": null,
          "script": null,
          "section": "reading",
          "seq": 21
        },
        {
          "answer": "od2959eef",
          "content_hash": "8ac16cf5d5010e035696ca5a34383d6b2374523a52400f9c2ffa622c50a0c5ff",
          "explanation": {
            "vi": "Đoạn cuối nhấn mạnh “how much depends on the design of the opening task”: bài toán mở đầu phải khó vừa phải, “the right kind of failure”. Phương án d bị câu cuối bác bỏ (không thể chỉ “withholding explanations and hoping for the best”); c là bẫy vì có cụm “bad at maths”, nhưng đó là hậu quả của bài toán quá khó chứ không phải nhóm học sinh bị loại trừ."
          },
          "id": "18d38299-ea22-57a4-b496-a176ad3692b7",
          "key": "en:examq:cefr-c1-m1-r12",
          "kind": "mcq",
          "level_code": "c1",
          "points": 1,
          "prompt": {
            "audio_asset_id": null,
            "instruction_vi": "Đọc bài bình luận và chọn câu thể hiện đúng quan điểm của người viết.",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "o64ffdb26",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "It works mainly because teachers explain less overall."
              },
              {
                "id": "ofa050593",
                "lang": "en",
                "text": "It is unsuitable for students who believe they are bad at maths."
              },
              {
                "id": "of556d9a2",
