            ],
            "passage": "For most of the last century, the standard model of teaching mathematics was simple: explain the method, demonstrate it with an example, then let students practise. A growing number of education researchers now argue that this sequence may be back to front. Under an approach known as \"productive failure\", students are first given a challenging problem they have not been taught how to solve. Most of them fail to reach the correct answer, often spectacularly. Only then does the teacher present the standard method.\nThe counter-intuitive claim is that the initial struggle is not wasted time. By attempting the problem on their own, students draw on what they already know, discover the limits of that knowledge and come to understand what a good solution would need to achieve. When the formal method is finally introduced, it answers a question they have already been asking. Several controlled studies have found that students taught in this way perform about as well on routine exercises as those taught conventionally, and noticeably better when asked to apply the idea to unfamiliar problems.\nEnthusiasts, however, sometimes overlook how much depends on the design of the opening task. A problem that is too easy leads to premature success and little reflection; one that is too hard leaves students with nothing to build on and simply confirms their belief that they are \"bad at maths\". The failure, in other words, has to be the right kind of failure. That is a demanding standard, and one that cannot be met by simply withholding explanations and hoping for the best.",
            "stem": "What does the writer suggest about the \"productive failure\" approach?",
